# Summary
A denial-of-service bug was introduced in the commit's ListObjectVersions delimiter implementation. The newly added delimiter handling skips common prefixes by iterating and refilling through all matching versions instead of using an indexed upper-bound seek or otherwise limiting work to the requested page size.
When ListObjectVersions is called with a delimiter and a page boundary lands on a CommonPrefix, the response uses that CommonPrefix as the next key marker. On the next request, the new delimiter path initializes that marker as an active common prefix and calls skip_cursor_prefix. That helper advances through cursor pages until it finds a key outside the prefix, refilling from SQLite each time. Unlike the ListObjects delimiter path, this code does not seek directly to the prefix upper bound, so max-keys no longer bounds per-request work. An attacker with ListBucketVersions permission, or an anonymous caller against a bucket that permits public listing, can repeatedly request a marker such as key-marker=largeprefix/&delimiter=/&max-keys=1 and force the server to scan all versions beneath largeprefix/ before returning a tiny response.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [7c84aa2](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/7c84aa2) (`Seek past version-list common prefixes`).

The fix makes `ListObjectVersions` delimiter pagination use the same upper-bound seek strategy as `ListObjects`. `ListObjectVersionsReq` now supports an inclusive `start_at` key lower bound, and the PgStore version-listing query can seek directly to `key >= object_key_prefix_upper_bound(common_prefix)`. The storage merge loop uses that seek when an active common prefix must be skipped, avoiding repeated cursor refills through every version under that prefix. If a prefix has no representable upper bound, the existing iterative fallback remains.

Regression coverage was added for the `start_at` lower bound, and existing coordinator/s3-tests version-listing delimiter and pagination coverage was rerun.

Validation after the fix:

- `cargo test -p storage list_object_versions -- --nocapture`
- `cargo test -p server-core list_object_versions_delimiter -- --nocapture`
- `cargo nextest run -p s3-tests --test versioning list_object_versions`
- `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
- `cargo nextest run -p s3-tests`

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm network reachability: HTTP ListObjectVersions accepts `delimiter`, `key-marker`, and `max-keys` and forwards them.
- [x] Confirm coordinator forwards delimiter and key marker to storage ListObjectVersions implementation.
- [x] Confirm storage initializes `active_common_prefix` from a key marker ending in the delimiter.
- [x] Confirm `skip_cursor_prefix` skips matching keys by incrementing the cursor and refilling pages rather than seeking to a prefix upper bound.
- [x] Dynamically demonstrate that increasing objects under the prefix increases backend page fetches while the response remains bounded by `max_keys=1`.
## Report
Validated the finding against commit 477547d351e9e836e1036ca0eaf9c307da4dd8b3. I first attempted a direct crash-style run of a targeted PoC test; it completed successfully rather than crashing, which is expected for an algorithmic DoS. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). I then used LLDB to trace/count the vulnerable storage calls.

Reachability/code evidence: the HTTP ListObjectVersions handler accepts attacker-controlled `delimiter`, `key-marker`, and `max-keys` and passes them to the coordinator at `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:2956-2988`. The coordinator forwards `delimiter` and `key_marker` to storage at `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/listing.rs:129-140`. In storage, `fetch_limit = max_keys + 1` is used for each cursor page and passed to `pg.list_object_versions` at `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:1842-1858`; `refill_cursor` repeatedly loads more pages at `request_ops.rs:1866-1874`; `skip_cursor_prefix` advances one version at a time while the key starts with the common prefix and refills as needed at `request_ops.rs:1877-1885`; a `key_marker` ending in the delimiter becomes `active_common_prefix` at `request_ops.rs:1908-1912`; and common prefix emission sets `next_key_marker` to the prefix at `request_ops.rs:1952-1958`, making the marker reachable from normal pagination.

Dynamic PoC: I added a targeted unit-test patch in `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs` that creates a one-PG local cluster, writes N objects under `largeprefix/` plus one later key `zzzz`, and calls the actual storage API: `list_object_versions_for_bucket(bucket, None, Some("/"), Some("largeprefix/"), None, 1)`. The direct run with N=12 returned a tiny response despite the large skipped prefix: `ARGMIN_POC prefix_count=12 versions=1 common_prefixes=0 truncated=false next_key_marker=None elapsed_ms=3`.

Debugger evidence: I ran LLDB with a breakpoint at `crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:6269`, the start of `PgStore::list_object_versions`, auto-continuing and listing hit counts. With `max_keys=1`, N=12 prefix objects produced `hit count = 7`; N=40 produced `hit count = 21`, while both requests returned only one version. This matches repeated cursor refills of roughly two returned versions per page and proves per-request work grows with the number of versions under the skipped common prefix, not with `max_keys`. Therefore the suspected denial-of-service is valid.

